insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('0d0eb269-1478-54a8-8af2-6b47abffc396', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["contain", "prepare", "imagine", "discover"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Portable video game consoles and other small electronic devices tend to _____ batteries that can’t be easily taken out and swapped for new ones. Environmental policy researcher Carl Dalhammar warns that when these internal batteries stop working, the devices are usually thrown away, becoming harmful waste.'),
  ('34f125d6-9c73-5074-a394-f04d68fbefd8', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["concerning", "comparable", "sympathetic", "deliberate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A team of archaeologists examined 150 spherically shaped limestone rocks called spheroids that date back about 1.4 million years, concluding that early hominins intentionally chipped away at rocks to form these spheroids over time. The fact that their attempt to make the stones as round as possible was _____ suggests that early hominins may have been more cognitively sophisticated than previously thought.'),
  ('accc6f8b-210b-574c-ab30-138fd45e2140', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'As used in the text, what does the word "assume" most nearly mean?', '["Infer", "Mimic", "Undertake", "Presuppose"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The following text is adapted from George Bernard Shaw’s 1905 play Major Barbara.
LADY BRITOMART: You must learn to face life seriously, Stephen. I really cannot bear the whole burden of our family affairs any longer. You must advise me: you must assume the responsibility. STEPHEN: I!
LADY BRITOMART: Yes, you, of course. You were 24 last June.'),
  ('940c5190-0681-528e-ae3a-725e7bd23852', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?', '["To give an overview of a particular situation that the narrator finds startling.", "To examine how the narrator and his friends each contributed to navigating a challenge.", "To present the narrator’s generalization along with supporting examples from a specific situation.", "To convey the narrator’s confidence that he understands the role expected of him in a group."]'::jsonb, 4, 'The following text is from Jerome K. Jerome’s 1889 novel Three Men in a Boat (To Say Nothing of the Dog). The narrator and two friends are taking a boat down the River Thames in England.

In a boat, I have always noticed that it is the fixed idea of each member of the crew that he is doing everything. Harris’s notion was, that it was he alone who had been working, and that both George and I had been imposing upon him. George, on the other hand, ridiculed the idea of Harris’s having done anything more than eat and sleep, and had a cast-iron opinion that it was he—George himself—who had done all the labour worth speaking of.'),
  ('18d7e0df-aab1-5d94-8c07-385c6d6f056d', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?', '["To note a new finding about the number of Saturn’s moons.", "To explain how the CFHT works.", "To describe the atmospheric conditions of Jupiter.", "To discuss the history of Hawaii."]'::jsonb, 5, 'Saturn is the first planet in our solar system to be discovered to have more than 100 moons orbiting around it. A team of astronomers using the Canada-France-Hawaii Telescope (CFHT) in Hawaii detected 62 undiscovered moons that were previously too small or too dim to see. Saturn now outranks Jupiter as the planet in our solar system with the most observed moons.'),
  ('d74fdc92-08cc-5611-9867-ec555c3a3007', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice best states the overall structure of the text?', '["The narrator expresses relief that a disagreement was resolved more expediently than expected and then indicates how the situation might have gone differently.", "The narrator comments on the fact that a character remains unaware of how he is viewed by others and then generalizes about the problem of learning others'' opinions of one’s own actions.", "The narrator summarizes an earlier event involving a specific character and then anticipates the later significance it will have for that character.", "The narrator implies that a character is not well liked by his neighbors and then uses an extended comparison to demonstrate why their negative opinion of him is largely justified."]'::jsonb, 6, 'The following text is from George Eliot’s 1857 short story "The Sad Fortunes of the Rev. Amos Barton." In the text, the narrator addresses the reader directly and alludes to a discussion among Rev. Amos Barton’s neighbors.

It was happy for the Rev. Amos Barton that he did not, like us, overhear the conversation recorded in the last chapter. Indeed, what mortal is there of us, who would find his satisfaction enhanced by an opportunity of comparing the picture he presents to himself of his own doings, with the picture they make on the mental retina of his neighbours? We are poor plants buoyed up by the air-vessels of our own conceit: alas for us, if we get a few pinches that empty us of that windy self-subsistence! The very capacity for good would go out of us.'),
  ('5685bb0a-b3a5-5579-9488-2b7af9a0653c', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It presents a scientific study that relied on a particular approach, then describes a barrier to the widespread adoption of that approach.", "It evaluates a research methodology used in a particular study, then explains how scientists may overcome difficulties inherent to that methodology.", "It summarizes the findings of a scientific study, then discusses the possibility of other researchers replicating those findings in future studies.", "It exemplifies a common method of genomic data analysis, then details the difficulties in adapting that method to new circumstances."]'::jsonb, 7, 'Allison Q. Byrne and colleagues relied on historical DNA (hDNA)—genomic data incidentally preserved in specimens housed in collections such as those at the Smithsonian National Museum of Natural History —to investigate the evolutionary origins of a pathogen affecting amphibians. Although this approach can yield many insights about the biological past, it remains a relatively underutilized resource in part because hDNA is often to some extent degraded, a situation not easily remediable under current methodological paradigms and with extant DNA extraction and analysis technologies.'),
  ('96aa1fe9-3c7b-50c2-a8c6-1a47d17dd92e', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely answer the underlined question in Text 1?', '["Although the programs may seem to produce positive results, close analysis of studies of the programs shows that they produce negative results just as frequently.", "It is unknown what results the programs produce because studies of them are often designed in ways that do not allow for definitive conclusions.", "Most of the programs do not actually produce positive results even though program participants tend to regard them as beneficial.", "The programs tend to produce inconsistent results because they vary substantially in their methods and aims."]'::jsonb, 8, '**Text 1**

Yale University and the investment bank JPMorgan Chase are two of the many institutions offering training programs in entrepreneurship. <u>But what results do such programs produce?</u> In a study of college students in Sweden, researcher Ove Hansemark addressed this question and found that participants showed strong belief in their entrepreneurial capabilities after receiving entrepreneurial training. **Text 2**

While studies of entrepreneurial training typically report positive results, a close look at these studies reveals widespread methodological shortcomings. For instance, a 1998 study by Ove Hansemark found benefits of entrepreneurial education for Swedish college students, but the study used a very small sample of only 19 students, making it difficult to say whether the training actually had an effect.'),
  ('408ea251-c330-5cbc-86ad-690b6a491abe', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'According to the text, Stephen Graham Jones and Cherie Dimaline are similar in what way?', '["Both have published fictional works featuring main characters who are Blackfeet.", "Both are citizens of the Blackfeet Nation.", "Both are Indigenous authors who have written horror fiction.", "Both have said that they don’t like reading horror fiction even though they write it."]'::jsonb, 9, 'The 2020 novel The Only Good Indians confirmed that Stephen Graham Jones is one of the most talented writers of horror fiction today. By featuring main characters who are Blackfeet, like Jones himself, the novel also helped to ensure that Indigenous people have a place within the horror genre. But Jones is hardly the only Indigenous voice in horror. Métis author Cherie Dimaline has also written in the genre. Her acclaimed 2019 novel Empire of Wild is set in a Métis community in southern Canada.'),
  ('b5d8f49a-060f-5161-ae8c-931960f1bd85', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Barragán’s designs of the Chávez Peón de Ochoa House and the Aguilar House are considered paragons of a functionalist and modernist aesthetic.", "A notable shift in Barragán’s design aesthetic reflects the influence of his time abroad.", "Barragán’s early work shows an initial dedication to a modernist aesthetic that he later abandoned.", "Barragán’s design of the Aguilar House is considered more experimental than his design for the Chávez Peón de Ochoa House."]'::jsonb, 10, 'Spanning the 1920s to the 1980s, Mexican architect Luis Barragán’s prolific career evolved through distinct phases. After traveling to the United States and Europe in the early 1930s and immersing himself in an international architectural discourse, Barragán began incorporating principles derived from functionalism and modernism in his work, as seen in the Chávez Peón de Ochoa House, whose unadorned geometric forms contrast with the historically inspired architecture found in the Aguila House, one of Barragán’s early projects in Guadalajara.'),
  ('f0359a91-5881-5fe0-b832-62a75f5140d1', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the assertion?', '["Wisconsin.", "Michigan.", "Florida.", "Indiana."]'::jsonb, 11, 'In 2021, Rocío Uría-Martínez, Megan M. Johnson, and Rul Shan published a report on hydroelectric power plants operating in the US as recently as 2019, and data from their report are shown in the table. Of the plants in the table, the plant with the lowest average power generated per year in 2019 was located in the state of _____

| Plant | State | Mode | Generators in plant | Average power generation (MWh/yr) | Water source |
| J. Woodruff | Florida | peaking | 3 | 193,864 | Lake Seminole Reservoir |
| Superior Falls | Michigan | run-of- river | 2 | 10,693 | Montreal River |
| Norway | Indiana | run-of- river | 4 | 19,751 | Tippecanoe River |
| White River | Wisconsin | run-of- river | 2 | 3,999 | White River |'),
  ('9f8dc54f-0868-53fa-b94e-6129259544f1', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the underlined claim?', '["Consumers tend to prefer new products over older products.", "Consumers are more likely to buy a new product when they already use products from the company making the new product.", "Consumer surveys show low excitement for new products that were announced a long time before they were released.", "The kind of information a company provides about a new product helps determine how likely consumers are to buy the product."]'::jsonb, 12, 'When a company has a new product, it has to decide when to tell people about it. Companies usually announce a new product before it is released and available for purchase. Those announcements can increase consumer excitement, which can mean that more people will buy the product. But <u>the effect</u> <u>fades quickly, so companies need to be careful in timing the announcement and the release of a new</u> <u>product.</u>'),
  ('3ddc5586-3c4a-5b67-8dfc-b758afd50861', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support Ehmann and colleagues'' hypothesis?', '["Individual 6 and individual 1 directed a higher proportion of peering events at permanent residents of their home regions than did individual 13 and individual 15.", "The proportion of peering events directed at permanent residents of the immature orangutans'' home regions ranged from a low of 0.00 to a high of 0.67.", "Individual 6 directed a higher proportion of peering events at permanent residents of its home region than did individual 13 directed a higher proportion of peering events at permanent residents of its home region than did individual 15.", "Individual 13 engaged in 27 peering events, more than either individual 6 or individual 1."]'::jsonb, 13, 'One way that young orangutans acquire foraging skills is through a behavior scientists call peering—closely watching older orangutans as they engage in an activity that the young have not yet mastered. Since female orangutans typically remain in the same area from youth through adulthood and males do not, Beatrice Ehmann and her colleagues hypothesized that it is more advantageous for immature females than males to devote attention to orangutans who are permanent residents of the immature individual’s home region, and this should be reflected in sex-specific differences in peering behavior.

| Individual | Site | Sex | Total number of peering events observed | Proportion of peering events directed at permanent residents of immature orangutan’s home region |
| 1 | Suaq | female | 17 | 0.59 |
| 13 | Tuanan | male | 27 | 0.15 |
| 15 | Tuanan | male | 15 | 0.00 |
| 6 | Tuanan | female | 6 | 0.67 |'),
  ('8e8d882b-bf0a-5e15-aba6-97c11fef7fa3', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which quotation from the article would best illustrate the theater historian’s claim?', '["\"The members of the company, which in addition to founder Luis Valdez consisted entirely of nonprofessional actors, traveled into farm fields, where they, with minimal props and costumes, performed comedy in the form of brief humorous vignettes.\"", "\"The company was focused on the reality of the present situation and discovered that humor was often found in that reality; consequently, comedy became a tool to convey social critique while entertaining and inspiring audiences.\"", "\"The company relied heavily on satire, humor and references to contemporary popular culture as well as make-do aesthetic—often referred to as rasquache—that reflected not only the troupe’s limited financial resources but also its sociopolitical message.\"", "\"The company presented actos, short comedy sketches, that often relied on exaggerated physical humor to groups of agricultural workers, whose reactions—enthusiastic cheers of appreciation and, occasionally, loud boos of disapproval—promoted audience participation.\""]'::jsonb, 14, 'Founded in 1965 and originally established as a cultural extension of the United Farm Workers—a union representing many Mexican American agricultural workers at the time—the theater troupe El Teatro Campesino has achieved recognition as a source of inspiration for subsequent Chicano theater companies and as a contributor to the dramatic arts. In an article about the company, a theater historian posits that a significant stylistic influence on El Teatro’s early performances was the audience-mediated slapstick comedy of carpa theater, vaudeville style shows popular in Mexico and the US Southwest in the 1920s and ''30s.'),
  ('0853a85e-e7d4-56af-9bd1-0006832c9ad3', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["share their observations about the unusual characteristics of edelweiss with scientists.", "encourage more flower enthusiasts to explore the Swiss Alps.", "make themselves appear brave and strong for being able to climb to difficult places where edelweiss supposedly grew.", "prove that edelweiss were more common in the Swiss Alps than in other mountain regions in Europe."]'::jsonb, 15, 'In Switzerland, the white fuzzy mountain flowers known as edelweiss are widely treated as a symbol of strength and courage. Although edelweiss can thrive in extreme conditions, they aren’t notably tougher or harder to reach than other mountain flowers growing in the Swiss Alps. Historian Tobias Scheidegger has shown that the popular view of the flowers originated in the mid-1800s when mountain climbing became popular in Switzerland. Mountain climbers spread the idea that the flowers grew only in steep, icy terrains that were dangerous to climb to. Scheidegger says that these claims were self-interested. He suggests that mountain climbers presented edelweiss in this way in order to _____'),
  ('c8994a5a-0490-5dd6-8068-41d6b6b91cd8', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["has been", "will have been", "was", "is"]'::jsonb, 16, 'A number of scientific phenomena have been named after nineteenth-century Czech physiologist Jan Evangelist Purkinje, including the Purkinje effect, which _____ the eye’s tendency to perceive objects as blue tinted in low-light conditions.'),
  ('e60e70e2-3e88-5628-8014-017adae36f55', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["brachialis are cylindrical in shape, which are skeletal muscles, and help", "brachialis are cylindrical in shape and help, which are skeletal muscles,", "brachialis, which are skeletal muscles, are cylindrical in shape and help", "brachialis are cylindrical, which are skeletal muscles, in shape and help"]'::jsonb, 17, 'The fibularis longus and the _____ move the fibula and humerus, respectively.'),
  ('25db10ff-7a4c-567a-9721-413b623634ee', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["Study of Two Peasants, the drawing, one", "Study of Two Peasants. The drawing, one", "Study of Two Peasants, the drawing. One", "Study of Two Peasants the drawing. One"]'::jsonb, 18, 'It was April of 1885 when Vincent van Gogh completed _____ of dozens to be featured in the upcoming exhibition, is an important part of Van Gogh’s body of work.'),
  ('244735b0-4a5f-5b16-bb89-58f6622b0a09', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["materials, that Palma Rodriguez uses in his art", "materials that Palma Rodriguez uses in his art", "materials that Palma Rodriguez uses in his art,", "materials, that Palma Rodriguez uses in his art,"]'::jsonb, 19, 'Fernando Palma Rodriguez creates robotic sculptures that combine mechanical elements with materials like feathers, soil, and seeds. The artist is from a rural farming community outside Mexico City, and he studied engineering in college. The natural and mechanical _____ highlight these two aspects of his background.'),
  ('0bcb8530-c4f0-58d7-a9e8-a1e91c82a66d', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["abound, in mathematics,", "abound in mathematics:", "abound: in mathematics,", "abound in mathematics;"]'::jsonb, 20, 'Exemplars of Stigler’s law _____ there is the Argand diagram; in astronomy, the Cassegrain reflector; and in physics, the Fermi paradox. All the aforementioned share the trait of being named after individuals who were not their initial discoverers or inventors.'),
  ('8ec01edf-d651-55ff-8b5d-b77acc570459', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Similarly,", "On the contrary,", "First of all,", "Next,"]'::jsonb, 21, 'The eruption of Rusty Geyser in Yellowstone National Park is caused by a sequence of events. First, water seeps down through narrow channels in the bedrock. _____ magma heats the water and turns it into steam. Finally, that steam builds up enough pressure in the narrow channels to force the water above it to burst out of the ground.'),
  ('d90dd52b-34a9-518b-a68c-ea00062265ac', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Concurrently,", "For example,", "In other words,", "Eventually,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'In the West African country of Sierra Leone, the percentage of the population living in cities rose from 40.8% to 42.9% between 2015 and 2020. _____ urbanization rates climbed across West Africa as a whole, rising 3.2 percentage points in that five-year period.'),
  ('0f0ee995-b32e-5535-a876-ee03b9070253', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'The student wants to contrast how the igneous rock at the two national parks formed. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Acadia National Park contains examples of intrusive igneous rock; the other type of igneous rock is called extrusive igneous rock.", "Acadia National Park’s igneous rock formed from molten rock that cooled deep within Earth’s crust, whereas Crater Lake National Park’s igneous rock formed from molten rock that cooled after it exited Earth’s crust.", "Acadia National Park and Crater Lake National Park both contain igneous rock, which is formed when molten rock cools.", "Two types of igneous rock, which is formed when molten rock cools, are intrusive igneous rock and extrusive igneous rock, both of which can be found in national parks."]'::jsonb, 23, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Igneous rock is one of the three main types of rock.
It is formed when molten rock, known as magma or lava, cools.
Intrusive igneous rock forms when molten rock cools deep within Earth’s crust.
Extrusive igneous rock forms when molten rock exits Earth’s crust and cools on the surface. Acadia National Park in Maine contains examples of intrusive igneous rock.
Crater Lake National Park in Oregon contains examples of extrusive igneous rock.'),
  ('53825365-1b01-55cf-ad0c-6b926ef88e32', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'The student wants to contrast the number of haiku in each poem. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["The poem \"15 haiku (for Toni Morrison)\" consists of fifteen haiku, whereas the poem \"7 haiku (for Ray Brown)\" consists of seven.", "Both \"15 haiku (for Toni Morrison)\" and \"7 haiku (for Ray Brown)\" can be found in Sanchez’s 2010 collection Morning Haiku.", "While \"15 haiku (for Toni Morrison)\" is about writer Toni Morrison, \"7 haiku (for Ray Brown)\" is about bassist Ray Brown.", "The poems in Morning Haiku celebrate the lives or mourn the deaths of \"revered African American figures,\" according to the book’s publisher, Penguin Random House."]'::jsonb, 24, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Morning Haiku (2010) is a book of poetry by African American poet Sonia Sanchez.
Each poem in the book is a sequence of haiku.
According to the book’s publisher, Penguin Random House, the book "celebrates the gifts of life and mourns the deaths of revered African American figures."
The poem "15 haiku (for Toni Morrison)" is written as a sequence of fifteen haiku.
The poem "7 haiku (for Ray Brown)" is written as a sequence of seven haiku.'),
  ('61fce89f-4934-58c4-b9da-974dd2bf5964', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'The student wants to indicate that both soundscapes are on the list. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Both the sound of crickets on the banks of the Yodo River and the sound of water flowing over Nachi Falls are on the list.", "The sound of water flowing over Nachi Falls is on the list.", "The Japanese Ministry of the Environment made a list of 100 culturally significant soundscapes of Japan.", "Each soundscape on the list, including the sound of crickets on the banks of the Yodo River, was selected for its cultural significance to Japan."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

The Japanese Ministry of the Environment made a list of 100 soundscapes of Japan.
Each soundscape on the list was selected for its cultural significance to Japan.
The sound of crickets on the banks of the Yodo River is on the list.
The sound of water flowing over Nachi Falls is on the list.'),
  ('16ec7969-d84a-517c-baca-a5174556ee6b', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'The student wants to describe Untitled (Topographic Landscape) to a new audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Artist Maya Lin is well known for her installation art, such as Untitled (Topographic Landscape) (1997), and for her memorials.", "Though Maya Lin’s Untitled (Topographic Landscape) (1997) is not a memorial, its gallery-filling scale may call to mind the imposing Vietnam Veterans Memorial, which consists of two 246- foot granite walls.", "Maya Lin’s Vietnam Veterans Memorial is a granite memorial sculpture that commemorates veterans of the Vietnam War, while Untitled (Topographic Landscape) is an installation artwork.", "Completed in 1997, Maya Lin’s Untitled (Topographic Landscape) is a large-scale installation artwork composed of wood that fills an entire gallery room."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Maya Lin is an American artist known for her memorials and works of installation art.
She completed the Vietnam Veterans Memorial in 1982.
It is a memorial sculpture consisting of two 246-foot granite walls, and it is designed to commemorate veterans of the Vietnam War.
She completed Untitled (Topographic Landscape) in 1997.
It is an installation composed of wood that fills an entire gallery room.'),
  ('c44906c5-794a-56a1-ab5e-e9e1b068b939', 'd6a65e7e-5757-553f-b6fb-e7c4fb4bfd2f', 'The student wants to explain Cummings and Knapp’s contribution to Lullfitz’s study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Lullfitz found that Cummings and Knapp had information relevant to her study of Platysace deflexa ’s distribution.", "Cummings and Knapp confirmed that DNA sequences from the Platysace deflexa plants Lullfitz had gathered were in fact very similar.", "Lullfitz consulted Cummings and Knapp, members of the Noongar Aboriginal group, who assisted with her study of Platysace deflexa , a small shrub with edible tubers.", "By indicating that their ancestors transported Platysace deflexa , Cummings and Knapp provided information that supported Lullfitz’s hypothesis."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Australian ecologist Alison Lullfitz studied the distribution of Platysace deflexa (youlk), a small shrub with edible tubers.
Lullfitz found that youlk plants gathered at sites across southwest Australia had very similar DNA despite differences in soil conditions.
Lullfitz hypothesized that humans had transported the plant throughout the region.
Lullfitz consulted Shandell Cummings and Lynette Knapp, members of the area’s Noongar Aboriginal group.
Cummings and Knapp confirmed that their ancestors carried youlk when traveling across the region.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('0d0eb269-1478-54a8-8af2-6b47abffc396', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence says these devices have batteries that "can’t be easily taken out and swapped for new ones," so the most logical verb is one that indicates possession: they "contain" such batteries. "Contain" precisely conveys that the devices have internal, nonremovable batteries, which leads directly to Dalhammar’s warning about disposal.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Prepare" does not make sense semantically: devices do not "prepare batteries" in the intended way and it does not describe possession or configuration of batteries inside the devices.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Imagine" is unrelated to the physical property being described; it cannot logically complete a factual statement about what devices have.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Discover" would imply someone finds batteries, which doesn’t fit the intended meaning that the devices include built-in batteries.
**Faster approach:** Use meaning elimination: substitute each choice into the sentence and choose the one that logically expresses possession or inclusion; for devices and batteries that will be thrown away, that word is "contain."'),
  ('34f125d6-9c73-5074-a394-f04d68fbefd8', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage argues that the hominins intentionally shaped stones, and saying their attempt “was deliberate” precisely conveys intentional, purposeful action—supporting the inference that they were cognitively sophisticated. "Deliberate" matches both sense and register of the sentence.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Concerning" means "worrisome," which conflicts with the positive inference about cognitive sophistication and does not describe the nature of the attempt to shape stones.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Comparable" means "similar to something else," which does not describe the quality or intent of the attempt and leaves the sentence semantically incomplete.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Sympathetic" means "compassionate" or "agreeable," which is irrelevant to describing how the hominins carried out the shaping of the stones.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the sentence and pick the one whose meaning logically implies intentional, purposeful action—here, "deliberate."'),
  ('accc6f8b-210b-574c-ab30-138fd45e2140', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** In the exchange, Lady Britomart tells Stephen, “You must assume the responsibility,” meaning he should take on or accept the duties of managing family affairs. "Assume" here functions as a transitive verb meaning to undertake a role or obligation.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Infer" means to draw a conclusion from evidence; that sense does not fit the command to take on duties.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Mimic" means to imitate someone’s behavior; Lady Britomart is asking Stephen to accept responsibility, not to imitate her.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Presuppose" means to take something for granted in advance; it does not convey accepting or performing a responsibility.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the sentence; the one that preserves the sentence’s meaning (here, "undertake") is the correct synonym.'),
  ('940c5190-0681-528e-ae3a-725e7bd23852', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage advances a general claim—that in a boat each crew member believes he alone has done all the work—and immediately supports that claim with two specific examples: Harris’s view that he did all the work and George’s opposing view that he did the real labour. The narrator’s observation frames the generalization and the subsequent sentences supply the supporting instances.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not emphasize the narrator being startled; it states an observed pattern and gives examples rather than portraying surprise.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The focus is not on how they jointly navigated a challenge or on the actual contributions each made, but on each person’s claim that he alone worked.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The narrator does not express confidence about understanding his expected role; instead he reports a general tendency and examples of others'' self-serving claims.
**Faster approach:** Identify the opening general statement (the claim) and check whether the rest of the passage gives examples or develops a single person’s perspective—if it gives examples supporting a generalization, choose the option describing that structure.'),
  ('18d7e0df-aab1-5d94-8c07-385c6d6f056d', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage reports a recent discovery that Saturn has more than 100 moons, describing that astronomers using the Canada–France–Hawaii Telescope detected 62 previously unseen moons and that Saturn now has more observed moons than Jupiter. The entire passage is focused on this new finding about the number of Saturn’s moons, so choice A best captures the main purpose.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage only mentions the CFHT in passing as the instrument used; it gives no explanation of how the telescope works, so B is not supported.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Jupiter’s atmospheric conditions are not discussed; Jupiter is mentioned only for comparison of moon counts.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Hawaii is noted only as the telescope’s location; there is no discussion of Hawaiian history.
**Faster approach:** Read the topic sentence and concluding sentence to identify the main fact or claim; that single-sentence summary usually matches the correct main-purpose choice.'),
  ('d74fdc92-08cc-5611-9867-ec555c3a3007', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage begins by noting that "It was happy for the Rev. Amos Barton that he did not...overhear the conversation," a specific observation about the character’s ignorance of others'' views, and then immediately broadens to a general claim about people who would not want to compare their self-image with how neighbors see them, calling us "poor plants" sustained by conceit. This matches a move from a particular example to a generalization about the problem of learning others'' opinions.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The text does not describe a resolved disagreement or emphasize an unexpectedly quick resolution.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not summarize an earlier event in detail or anticipate later significance; it uses the example only to introduce a general observation.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The narrator does not argue that neighbors dislike Amos nor justify a negative opinion; the focus is on the hazard of hearing others'' judgments.
**Faster approach:** Spot the opening specific reference and then note whether the next sentences generalize; that pattern indicates an example-to-generalization structure (choice B).'),
  ('5685bb0a-b3a5-5579-9488-2b7af9a0653c', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage first describes a specific scientific study in which Allison Q. Byrne and colleagues used historical DNA (hDNA) to investigate a pathogen’s evolutionary origins, and then immediately states a barrier to the broader use of that approach—namely, that hDNA is often degraded and current extraction/analysis technologies make remediation difficult. This sequence (study description followed by a limitation to wider adoption) matches choice A.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does characterize a method and its difficulty, but it does not explain ways scientists might overcome those difficulties; in fact it says the problem is "not easily remediable."
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not summarize study findings nor discuss replication by other researchers; it focuses on the method and a barrier to its use.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage does describe difficulties, but it does not present the method as a "common" one nor discuss adapting it to new circumstances; it emphasizes that the method is underutilized because of degradation issues.
**Faster approach:** Identify the order of ideas—what the first sentence does (introduce study/method) and what the next sentence does (state a limitation)—and choose the answer that matches that sequence.'),
  ('96aa1fe9-3c7b-50c2-a8c6-1a47d17dd92e', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** Text 2 emphasizes that studies of entrepreneurial training have “widespread methodological shortcomings” and gives the specific example that Hansemark’s study had a sample of only 19 students, which makes it “difficult to say whether the training actually had an effect.” That language indicates the Text 2 author would respond to Text 1’s question by saying the results are essentially unknown because the studies are not designed to support definitive conclusions.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Text 2 does not claim studies show negative results as often as positive ones; it questions the studies’ design and the reliability of their conclusions rather than asserting frequent negative outcomes.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Text 2 does not assert that most programs fail to produce positive results; it emphasizes uncertainty caused by weak study designs, not evidence of widespread failure.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Text 2 criticizes study methodology (small samples, etc.), not variation among program methods and aims; the passage focuses on study quality rather than inconsistent program effects.
**Faster approach:** Look for the critic’s central claim—words like “methodological shortcomings” and “difficult to say” signal that the author is arguing the evidence is inconclusive, so choose the answer about uncertainty.'),
  ('408ea251-c330-5cbc-86ad-690b6a491abe', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage states that Stephen Graham Jones wrote the novel The Only Good Indians, a work of horror fiction, and that Cherie Dimaline "has also written in the genre"—referring to horror—while identifying Dimaline as Métis. Thus both are Indigenous authors who have written horror fiction.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage says Jones features Blackfeet main characters but specifies Dimaline is Métis; it does not say Dimaline wrote works with Blackfeet main characters.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Only Jones is described as Blackfeet; Dimaline is identified as Métis, not a member of the Blackfeet Nation.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage gives no information about either author’s personal reading preferences or whether they dislike reading horror.
**Faster approach:** Scan the sentence that names each author and note the descriptors (identity and genre). Match the shared descriptor between the two names to eliminate incorrect choices quickly.'),
  ('b5d8f49a-060f-5161-ae8c-931960f1bd85', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage emphasizes a change in Barragán’s aesthetic after travel: it says that after visiting the United States and Europe he began "incorporating principles derived from functionalism and modernism," and contrasts the unadorned geometric forms of the Chávez Peón de Ochoa House with the "historically inspired architecture" of his earlier Aguila House, indicating a notable shift caused by his time abroad.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage contrasts the Chávez Peón de Ochoa House (modernist/functional) with the Aguila House (historically inspired), so it does not present both as paragons of the same modernist aesthetic.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage describes early work as historically inspired and a later incorporation of modernist principles, not an initial modernism that was later abandoned.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage does not describe the Aguila House as more experimental; it characterizes the Aguila House as historically inspired in contrast to the modernist Chávez Peón de Ochoa House.
**Faster approach:** Look for timing and transition words (for example, "after" and contrasts); identify whether the passage describes change over time, then eliminate choices that reverse or misstate that change.'),
  ('f0359a91-5881-5fe0-b832-62a75f5140d1', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The table lists the average power generation (MWh/yr) for each plant: J. Woodruff (Florida) 193,864; Superior Falls (Michigan) 10,693; Norway (Indiana) 19,751; White River (Wisconsin) 3,999. The smallest value is 3,999 MWh/yr, which corresponds to the White River plant in Wisconsin, so Wisconsin completes the assertion correctly.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Michigan’s plant (Superior Falls) generated 10,693 MWh/yr, which is greater than Wisconsin’s 3,999 MWh/yr.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Florida’s J. Woodruff generated 193,864 MWh/yr, far larger than the lowest value.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Indiana’s Norway plant generated 19,751 MWh/yr, also larger than Wisconsin’s 3,999 MWh/yr.
**Faster approach:** Scan the "Average power generation" column and pick the smallest numeric value; then read its corresponding state in the same row.'),
  ('9f8dc54f-0868-53fa-b94e-6129259544f1', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The underlined claim says the positive effect of announcing a product "fades quickly," so timing matters. Choice C states that consumer excitement is low when announcements precede release by a long time, which directly shows the effect diminishes as the time gap increases and therefore supports the need for careful timing.
**Choice A is incorrect.** That consumers prefer new products generally might explain why announcements help, but it does not address whether the effect fades quickly or depends on timing.
**Choice B is incorrect.** That existing users are more likely to buy relates to who will purchase, not to how announcement timing affects excitement or how quickly the effect fades.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The kind of information affects purchase likelihood, but this speaks to message content rather than the speed with which announcement effects decline over time.
**Faster approach:** Look for the choice that explicitly links the effect to time (e.g., long delay or quick fade). For timing/effect claims, immediately eliminate options that discuss content or buyer type rather than time.'),
  ('3ddc5586-3c4a-5b67-8dfc-b758afd50861', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The hypothesis predicts that immature females will direct a higher proportion of peering events at permanent residents than immature males. The table shows females (individuals 1 and 6) have proportions 0.59 and 0.67, respectively, while males (individuals 13 and 15) have proportions 0.15 and 0.00; thus individuals 6 and 1 directed a higher proportion at permanent residents than did individuals 13 and 15.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although the table does show a range from 0.00 to 0.67, this statement does not address the sex-specific comparison that supports the hypothesis.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The choice is worded confusingly and duplicates a clause; moreover it compares individuals 6 and 13 to 15 in a way that doesn’t clearly state the relevant female-vs-male relationship.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While individual 13 did engage in 27 peering events (more than individuals 6 and 1), the hypothesis concerns proportions directed at permanent residents, not the total number of peering events.
**Faster approach:** Compare the proportions for females vs. males directly in the table—if all female proportions exceed all male proportions, the data support the hypothesis. This avoids focusing on raw counts or ranges.'),
  ('8e8d882b-bf0a-5e15-aba6-97c11fef7fa3', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The historian’s claim emphasizes slapstick (exaggerated physical humor) and audience mediation (audience responses shaping the performance). Choice D explicitly names "exaggerated physical humor" and describes audience reactions—"enthusiastic cheers... and... loud boos... promoted audience participation," which directly matches both elements of the claim.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it notes brief humorous vignettes performed with minimal props, it does not mention physical slapstick or audience-mediated interaction central to the historian’s claim.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This emphasizes humor as social critique and inspiration but does not describe slapstick or active audience participation.
**Choice C is incorrect.** While it describes satire, humor, and a make-do aesthetic, it does not mention exaggerated physical comedy or how audience reactions mediated performances.
**Faster approach:** Scan choices for the two key terms from the claim—physical/slapstick comedy and audience participation—and pick the option that explicitly includes both.'),
  ('0853a85e-e7d4-56af-9bd1-0006832c9ad3', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage says mountain climbers "spread the idea that the flowers grew only in steep, icy terrains that were dangerous to climb to" and that these claims were "self-interested." Presenting the flowers as reachable only by dangerous climbs would make those climbers seem especially brave and skilled, so claiming the flowers grew only in hazardous places serves to enhance the climbers'' reputation for courage and strength.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage gives no indication that climbers wanted to share botanical observations with scientists; it emphasizes self-interest tied to reputation, not scientific reporting.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Encouraging more flower enthusiasts to explore the Alps would not be an obviously self-interested motive for the climbers’ claims, nor does the passage suggest it.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage does not claim climbers aimed to show edelweiss were more common in Switzerland than elsewhere; rather, they exaggerated the difficulty of reaching them.
**Faster approach:** Look for the phrase indicating motive (here, "self-interested") and choose the option that ties that motive to personal gain—e.g., enhancing the climbers’ image or status.'),
  ('c8994a5a-0490-5dd6-8068-41d6b6b91cd8', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The clause beginning with "which" is a nonrestrictive relative clause that defines what the Purkinje effect does in general: it describes the eye’s tendency in low light. That tendency is a present, ongoing fact, so the simple present verb "is" correctly states the general truth.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Has been" suggests a present-perfect action or state that began in the past; it would imply the Purkinje effect "has been the eye’s tendency," which is awkward and ungrammatical for describing what the effect does.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Will have been" places the action in the future perfect and is inappropriate because the sentence describes a known, general characteristic, not a future condition.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Was" would limit the tendency to the past, implying it no longer holds; but the Purkinje effect describes a continuing phenomenon, so the simple present is required.
**Faster approach:** For clauses that state general truths or ongoing facts, use the simple present. Eliminate past or perfect tenses when the meaning is timeless or still true.'),
  ('e60e70e2-3e88-5628-8014-017adae36f55', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The phrase "brachialis, which are skeletal muscles, are cylindrical in shape and help" correctly places the nonrestrictive clause "which are skeletal muscles" next to its antecedent, maintains subject-verb agreement for the compound subject "The fibularis longus and the brachialis," and leads naturally into the verb phrase "help move the fibula and humerus, respectively." The sentence is grammatical and clear.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Placing "which are skeletal muscles" after the predicate separates the modifier from its noun and creates a comma splice; the structure is awkward and ungrammatical.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The clause "which are skeletal muscles" is tacked on at the end, separated from its noun, and the flow of the sentence is disrupted; word order is incorrect.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The phrase "which are skeletal muscles" interrupts the adjective phrase "are cylindrical in shape" in a way that makes the sentence ungrammatical and unclear.
**Faster approach:** Locate any nonrestrictive modifier ("which" clause) and ensure it directly follows its noun and that the main verb agrees with the full subject; eliminate choices that separate modifiers from their nouns or break subject-verb agreement.'),
  ('25db10ff-7a4c-567a-9721-413b623634ee', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** Placing a period after the title creates two sentences: "It was April of 1885 when Vincent van Gogh completed Study of Two Peasants." followed by "The drawing, one of dozens to be featured in the upcoming exhibition, is an important part of Van Gogh’s body of work." The second sentence correctly uses commas to set off the parenthetical phrase "one of dozens to be featured in the upcoming exhibition," and the subject "The drawing" agrees with the verb "is."
**Choice A is incorrect.** Setting off the title with a comma produces a single fused construction that leaves the clause "is an important part..." without a proper subject or verb agreement, creating a run-on/awkward sentence.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Ending the first sentence with a comma before "the drawing" then starting "One of dozens..." would create a sentence fragment or incorrect separation of the appositive; the appositive should be part of the sentence describing "The drawing."
**Choice D is incorrect.** Omitting punctuation between the title and "the drawing" and then starting a new sentence with "One" produces either a run-on or a fragment and fails to set off the appositive correctly.
**Faster approach:** Look for punctuation that cleanly separates the statement about completion from the descriptive appositive; convert the phrasing into two sentences—title ends the first sentence, then start the second with "The drawing," followed by the parenthetical set off by commas.'),
  ('244735b0-4a5f-5b16-bb89-58f6622b0a09', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The clause "that Palma Rodriguez uses in his art" is restrictive (it specifies which materials), so it should not be set off with commas. The sentence should read: "The natural and mechanical materials that Palma Rodriguez uses in his art highlight these two aspects of his background." This is concise and grammatically standard.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It incorrectly inserts a comma before the restrictive "that" clause, which breaks the flow and implies a nonrestrictive clause, creating a grammatical error.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It places a comma after the clause, which is unnecessary and disrupts the sentence; no punctuation is needed there.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It uses commas both before and after the clause, treating it as nonrestrictive; but the clause is essential to identify the materials, so commas are wrong.
**Faster approach:** Recognize that "that" introduces a restrictive clause (no commas). Eliminate any choice that adds commas around a "that" clause; the remaining option is correct.'),
  ('0bcb8530-c4f0-58d7-a9e8-a1e91c82a66d', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** A colon is required after the introductory clause because the sentence presents a list of exemplars that follows logically from that clause. The structure is "Exemplars of Stigler’s law _____ there is the Argand diagram; in astronomy, the Cassegrain reflector; and in physics, the Fermi paradox." The clause functions as an independent lead-in that announces examples, so a colon before the list is correct: "abound: in mathematics, there is the Argand diagram; in astronomy, the Cassegrain reflector; and in physics, the Fermi paradox."
**Choice A is incorrect.** A comma after "abound" produces a comma splice or a sentence fragment problem: the following material is an independent list of examples that should be introduced with stronger punctuation (a colon), not merely a comma.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Using a colon after "mathematics" (i.e., "abound in mathematics:") would place the colon in the middle of the prepositional phrase and create an awkward break; the intended colon must come immediately after the verb phrase "abound" that introduces the examples.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A semicolon after "mathematics" would incorrectly join the lead-in to the list; semicolons separate independent clauses or complex list items, but they do not serve as the conventional marker that introduces a list of examples the way a colon does.
**Faster approach:** When a sentence introduces a list of examples after an independent clause, immediately choose a colon. If the blank precedes a list or series that explains or exemplifies that clause, the correct punctuation is a colon.'),
  ('8ec01edf-d651-55ff-8b5d-b77acc570459', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The paragraph describes a sequence of events (first, then, finally), so the transitional word should indicate the next step; "Next," appropriately signals the intermediate action that follows the initial seepage and precedes the final eruption.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Similarly," indicates comparison to a previous idea rather than a chronological next step, so it does not fit the sequence of events.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "On the contrary," introduces a contrast or contradiction, which is not appropriate in a passage describing successive steps that lead to an outcome.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "First of all," repeats the notion of the first step even though the paragraph already begins with "First," making it redundant and disrupting the chronological flow.
**Faster approach:** For sequence descriptions, choose a chronological transition (Next, Then, Finally); eliminate choices that signal comparison or contrast.'),
  ('d90dd52b-34a9-518b-a68c-ea00062265ac', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence that follows reports a similar numerical change "in that five-year period," so a transition meaning "at the same time" is required. "Concurrently," signals that the rise in Sierra Leone occurred at the same time that urbanization rates climbed across West Africa as a whole, which fits the temporal link between the two clauses.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "For example," would introduce a specific instance illustrating a general claim; here the second clause is not an example but a parallel, region-wide change occurring in the same period.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "In other words," indicates a restatement or clarification of the preceding idea; the second clause provides a related but separate regional statistic, not a paraphrase of the Sierra Leone figure.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Eventually," implies a change occurring later, which contradicts the explicit timing "in that five-year period" and does not show simultaneity.
**Faster approach:** Look for timing words (e.g., "in that five-year period"); if both clauses refer to the same time frame, choose a transition meaning "at the same time" (e.g., "concurrently"/"simultaneously").'),
  ('0f0ee995-b32e-5535-a876-ee03b9070253', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It directly contrasts how the igneous rock at the two parks formed using the student’s notes: Acadia contains intrusive igneous rock that formed when molten rock cooled deep within Earth’s crust, whereas Crater Lake contains extrusive igneous rock that formed when molten rock cooled after exiting Earth’s crust. The sentence uses both park names and explicitly compares their formation processes, which matches the notes.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It identifies Acadia’s rock as intrusive and names the other type as extrusive but does not explain how the two types formed or contrast the parks’ formation processes.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It states a general fact that both parks contain igneous rock and that igneous rock forms when molten rock cools, but it fails to contrast how the rocks at the two parks formed.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It restates that there are two types of igneous rock and that they can be found in national parks but does not specifically contrast the formation processes at Acadia and Crater Lake.
**Faster approach:** For contrast questions, pick the answer that names both items and explicitly states opposing processes (look for words like “whereas” or paired descriptions of how each formed).'),
  ('53825365-1b01-55cf-ad0c-6b926ef88e32', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The student’s notes state that the poem “15 haiku (for Toni Morrison)” is written as a sequence of fifteen haiku and that “7 haiku (for Ray Brown)” is written as a sequence of seven haiku; restating those numbers side by side directly contrasts the counts in the two poems.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although it correctly locates both poems in Morning Haiku, it does not contrast the number of haiku in each poem.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The notes do not provide information about the poems’ subjects beyond their titles, and this choice does not compare the number of haiku.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This summarizes the publisher’s general claim about the collection but does not contrast the specific haiku counts of the two poems.
**Faster approach:** Scan the notes for numeric details and pick the option that places those numbers side by side to make a direct comparison.'),
  ('61fce89f-4934-58c4-b9da-974dd2bf5964', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** It explicitly states that both the sound of crickets on the banks of the Yodo River and the sound of water flowing over Nachi Falls are on the list, directly using the two relevant notes to indicate that both specific soundscapes are included.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It mentions only the sound of water flowing over Nachi Falls and therefore fails to indicate that the sound of crickets on the Yodo River is also on the list.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although it accurately summarizes the list’s origin and purpose, it does not mention either of the two specific soundscapes and so does not show that both are on the list.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It emphasizes that each listed soundscape was chosen for cultural significance and names the crickets, but it does not explicitly state that the sound of water flowing over Nachi Falls is on the list.
**Faster approach:** For synthesis questions asking whether two specific items are included, choose the answer that names both items together; this immediately confirms inclusion of both.'),
  ('16ec7969-d84a-517c-baca-a5174556ee6b', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** It directly and efficiently describes Untitled (Topographic Landscape) using facts from the notes: the completion year (1997), the medium (wood), and the notable scale (fills an entire gallery room). These details give a new audience a clear, concrete sense of the work.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although true, it is general and emphasizes Maya Lin’s overall reputation rather than giving specific descriptive details about Untitled (Topographic Landscape) for a new audience.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It introduces an unnecessary comparison to the Vietnam Veterans Memorial and suggests a subjective impression (“may call to mind”), which is not as clear or directly descriptive as the factual details available.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It correctly contrasts the two works but gives only a minimal label for Untitled (Topographic Landscape) (“an installation artwork”) without the specific medium, date, or scale that would best inform a new audience.
**Faster approach:** When asked to describe something, pick the answer that states concrete, relevant facts from the notes (date, medium, and size/scale) rather than generalizations or comparisons.'),
  ('c44906c5-794a-56a1-ab5e-e9e1b068b939', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The notes state that Lullfitz hypothesized humans had transported youlk and that Cummings and Knapp "confirmed that their ancestors carried youlk when traveling across the region." By reporting ancestral transport, Cummings and Knapp provided direct information that supports Lullfitz’s hypothesis, so this choice accurately and specifically describes their contribution.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It is vague: although true that they had relevant information, this choice doesn’t specify what that information was or how it related to Lullfitz’s hypothesis.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The similar DNA finding was Lullfitz’s result from plant samples, not something Cummings and Knapp confirmed; the notes say they confirmed ancestral transport, not DNA data.
**Choice C is incorrect.** While true that Lullfitz consulted them and they assisted, this statement fails to say that they confirmed ancestral transport or that their testimony supported the hypothesis.
**Faster approach:** Look for the note that directly connects the experts’ contribution to the researcher’s hypothesis—pick the choice that says they confirmed ancestral transport, which directly supports the hypothesis.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;

