insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('a9ab378d-7928-51c7-a9f8-cc80d84712c7', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["superficial", "myriad", "deficient", "untenable"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Some pieces of music might have _____ meanings—the compositions of Rosa Guraieb lend themselves to as many different interpretations as there are people to listen to them—and so as long as a listener’s interpretation isn’t willfully absurd or the result of inattention, it is difficult to justly claim that the listener has misunderstood the piece.'),
  ('565cb9fa-0633-509c-a103-3794f720791d', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'As used in the text, what does the word "confirmed" most nearly mean?', '["Necessitated", "Substantiated", "Designated", "Promised"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The following text is adapted from Neera’s 1886 novel Teresa, translated by Martha King in 1998. Beyond the [porch] extended a piece of land, with some exaggeration called a garden. In truth, it had some flower beds that at first sight <u>confirmed</u> the illusion, particularly at that time of year, since the pansies were in bloom with their infinite shades, with the intense velvet of their dark leaves and the luminous silk of their pale leaves.

©1976 by Giulio Einaudi editore s.p.a.

Translation ©1998 by Northwestern University Press.'),
  ('399f40fc-a6f3-5250-9ca6-7cf4d0cfe40a', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["satisfy", "simplify", "overtake", "undermine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'If confirmed by other researchers, a newly reported measurement of W boson’s mass could ____ the dominant theory of particle physics, the standard model, as the new measurement differs significantly from the standard model’s prediction of the elementary particle’s mass.'),
  ('9a40065e-7a3a-5ef2-bb4e-31c18cafd2e0', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["conflate", "epitomize", "lambaste", "overshadow"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Theater and film scholars often draw parallels between the lives of performers and aspects of roles they''ve played; however, the most insightful of these discussions, such as Linda Costanzo Cahir’s consideration of resemblances between actor Vivien Leigh and the character Blanche DuBois, maintain a rigid distinction between the actor and the character, taking care never to _____ them.'),
  ('3c0e0442-7d9b-5c7d-8161-414b338d4fce', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["accessible", "complex", "critical", "respectable"]'::jsonb, 5, 'Some social scientists argue that while a belief in the importance of reason and progress is key to democracy, the public’s understanding of history is also central to its subsequent comprehension of a state’s politics, and if an electorate is to function, historical issues cannot remain the dominion only of academics. History is too _____ to leave to historians alone.'),
  ('51b8bd97-1c24-5516-ae55-7f537298b77c', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It identifies the most frequently occurring words in Muscogee, explains why it is difficult to translate those words into English, and then provides examples of languages other than English into which those words can be translated.", "It explains the phenomenon of reduplication, discusses why reduplication has been controversial among scholars, and then argues that an analysis of Muscogee could help resolve that controversy.", "It describes the relationship between Muscogee and several other languages, raises a question about the nature of that relationship, and then answers that question.", "It presents some specific words in Muscogee, describes the general linguistic phenomenon exemplified by those words, and then states that this phenomenon occurs frequently in Muscogee."]'::jsonb, 6, 'In Muscogee, an Indigenous language from the southeastern region of what is now the United States, hatki means "white," whereas hathaki is used to refer to two or more white things. This phenomenon, in which an element of a root word is repeated, sometimes with modification, within another word that is related to the root word, is called reduplication. In this case, the element "ha" in hatki gets repeated in hathaki. There are many examples of this type of reduplication in Muscogee.'),
  ('0d78f499-d099-5259-9b9d-72fdd817acfe', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice best states the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?', '["It introduces researchers who will be discussed in greater detail later in the text.", "It gives an example of how some scientists had studied a phenomenon before another study mentioned later in the text was conducted.", "It outlines a study that was influenced by the researchers mentioned later in the text.", "It clarifies a concept that is unclear in some of the studies mentioned in the text."]'::jsonb, 7, '<u>Benjamin Prudhomme and colleagues have explored how convergent evolution—a phenomenon that</u> <u>occurs when the same trait evolves independently in two reproductively separate lineages—can result</u> <u>from a genetic mechanism shared by both lineages.</u> Meanwhile, Cynthia C. Steiner and colleagues have investigated how convergence occurs through different genetic mechanisms. However, the relative prevalence of convergence through shared and different genetic processes is still poorly understood. This motivated biologists Delbert A. Green II and Cassandra G. Extavour to evaluate both types of convergence in a single study for their 2012 paper.'),
  ('f7f57e94-a2f5-585a-af06-f46ade0e0dd7', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'According to the text, which choice most accurately describes the importance of the percentage of compact myocardial tissue in the basking shark’s heart?', '["It is insufficient to establish that the basking shark should be categorized as Dolton and colleagues suggest.", "It is higher than that of the salmon shark, a regional endotherm, despite the basking shark being a full ectotherm.", "It is higher than that of the Pacific bluefin tuna, a regional endotherm, which calls into question the basking shark’s status as a regional endotherm.", "It is, by itself, sufficient to invalidate how the basking shark has been categorized in the past."]'::jsonb, 8, 'The blue shark and the striped marlin are ectothermic (cold-blooded) fish, whereas the salmon shark and the Pacific bluefin tuna are regional endotherms—they retain metabolic heat resulting in body temperatures above the ambient water temperature. Unlike those of ectotherms, regional endotherms'' hearts have a relatively high proportion (greater than 30%) of compact myocardial tissue, which is needed to maintain these animals'' blood pressure. In a 2023 study, Haley R. Dolton and colleagues found that basking sharks—planktivorous filter-feeders that were classified as full ectotherms at the time—have hearts consisting of 47% compact myocardial tissue, thereby undermining that classification.'),
  ('5ad37b62-4c3b-544f-8e33-f75874e4b8f1', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Pallikkathayil’s argument suggests that if we have a duty to respect other people’s agential interests and if possession of false beliefs constrains agency, then we have an inviolable duty to speak truthfully.", "Pallikkathayil’s argument shows that if our intuition that circumstances may make lying permissible is correct, then it is unclear whether there are any grounds for an opposition to lying in the first place.", "One potential means of ing opposition to lying is Pallikkathayil’s argument that we have an obligation to respect other people’s agency that entails a commitment to truthfulness except in certain circumstances.", "Many people have an intuitive sense that lying is permissible in some circumstances but lack a principled way to identify those circumstances, and Pallikkathayil’s argument may provide a means of resolving that problem."]'::jsonb, 9, 'Philosophers note that many people have an intuitive sense that while we ought not to lie, there may be circumstances in which lying is permissible. If this intuition is correct and we lack an inviolable duty to speak truthfully, what grounds opposition to lying in the first place? Japa Pallikkathayil has advanced one answer by appealing to a duty to respect others'' agential interests: the possession of false beliefs constrains agency, and thus we ought not to impede the formation of true beliefs unless doing so prevents a greater constraint on someone’s agency or an otherwise impermissible end.'),
  ('dc1fe7b4-9a39-5804-8577-3c7e2617e9da', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the second research team’s hypothesis?', '["The longnose gar (Lepisosteus osseus) has a much narrower head than the devil catfish (Bagarius bagarius), and the most sensitive sensors of the longnose gar are closer to the snout than are those of the devil catfish.", "The longnose gar (Lepisosteus osseus) has a much narrower head than the devil catfish (Bagarius bagarius), and the most sensitive sensors for both are very close to their snouts.", "The longnose gar (Lepisosteus osseus) has a much narrower head than the devil catfish (Bagarius bagarius), and while the sensors nearest the snout for the longnose gar are more sensitive than the others, for the devil catfish all sensors are equally sensitive.", "The longnose gar (Lepisosteus osseus) has a much narrower head than the devil catfish (Bagarius bagarius), and the most sensitive sensors for the devil catfish are closer to the snout than are those for the longnose gar."]'::jsonb, 10, 'To detect information about water flow, fish have sensors running from the snout tip down the sides of the head. Yuzo R. Yanagisuru, Otar Akanyeti, and James C. Liao conducted tests to find where the difference in pressure is greatest between two adjacent sensors because, according to the researchers, as these pressure differences increase, so does the amount of information available to the fish. Using the ratio of head width to length, they found that the greatest pressure difference is closer to the snout for narrower heads (lower ratio of width to length) and farther from the snout for wider heads (higher ratio of width to length). Based on this finding, a second team of researchers has hypothesized that the sensors where information is greatest are likely more sensitive than the rest.'),
  ('ab0f8920-e034-5004-9ffc-e9c7fe215be7', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the assertion?', '["both geothermal and wind neither increased nor decreased from 2019 to 2020.", "both wind and solar never surpassed that of renewable hydropower throughout the four-year period.", "wind was much lower in 2017 than it was in 2018, 2019, or 2020.", "renewable hydropower was much higher than that of solar for all four years."]'::jsonb, 11, 'Electricity Capacity Trends (in megawatts) for Four Renewable Technologies in Indonesia (2017-2020)

| Energy | 2017 | 2018 | 2019 | 2020 |
| Geothermal | 1,808 | 1,948 | 2,131 | 2,131 |
| Renewable hydropower | 5,703 | 5,773 | 5,976 | 6,141 |
| Solar | 97.4 | 65.5 | 155 | 185.3 |
| Wind | 1.5 | 143.5 | 154.3 | 154.3 |

Indonesia is trying to increase its electricity capacity (the maximum amount of electricity that can be generated) for renewable energy in order to reduce dependence on fossil fuels, which can be costly financially and environmentally. From 2017 to 2020, Indonesia’s use of four renewable technologies has trended upward, but not uniformly: the electricity capacity of solar power fell from 97.4 megawatts in 2017 to 65.5 megawatts in 2018, and the electricity capacity of ____'),
  ('ce42085c-d751-5b71-b6f3-5a82aba2547c', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the researchers'' conclusion?', '["At both Tiffany & Co. and Forever 21, participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be less valuable than jewelry spaced close together, but the difference in perceived value was significantly greater at Tiffany & Co. than at Forever 21.", "When jewelry was spaced far apart, participants judged the jewelry at Tiffany & Co. to be more valuable than the jewelry at Forever 21, but when jewelry was spaced close together, participants judged the jewelry at Tiffany & Co. to be less valuable than the jewelry at Forever 21.", "At Tiffany & Co., participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be substantially more valuable than jewelry spaced close together, but at Forever 21, participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be only slightly more valuable than jewelry spaced close together.", "Participants judged jewelry spaced far apart at Tiffany & Co. to be similar in value to jewelry spaced far apart at Forever 21, but participants judged jewelry spaced close together at Tiffany & Co. to be more valuable than jewelry spaced close together at Forever 21."]'::jsonb, 12, 'In a series of experiments, Julio Sevilla and Claudia Townsend showed that manipulating the space between products in store displays can influence consumers'' views of those products. Participants in several of the experiments regarded the same products in the same (generic) retail settings as significantly more valuable when the product-to-space ratio was low than when it was high. But in one of the experiments, Sevilla and Townsend arranged the same jewelry with different levels of intervening space at an upscale retailer (Tiffany & Co.) and a relatively inexpensive retailer (Forever 21). The result of this experiment suggests that a store context associated with inexpensive products may moderate the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their other experiments.'),
  ('dc41bd0c-4625-588f-9e27-80fee202df9f', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice most effectively uses a quotation from a translation of The Clouds to illustrate the claim?', '["Strepsiades, after taking lessons from a sophist, says to his son, \"Approach, that you may know more; and I will tell you a thing, by learning which you will be a man. But see that you do not teach this to any one.\"", "Socrates, a sophist, says to a potential customer, \"I wish to briefly learn from you if you are possessed of a good memory.\"", "Strepsiades encourages his son to learn to be a sophist, saying, \"If you have any concern for your father’s patrimony, become one of them.\"", "Socrates, a sophist, explains why he studies astronomy while sitting in a basket hanging a few feet off the ground, saying, \"I should not have rightly discovered things celestial if I had not suspended the intellect, and mixed the thought in a subtle form with its kindred air.\""]'::jsonb, 13, 'The Clouds is a 423 BCE play by Aristophanes, originally written in ancient Greek. At the time, professional intellectuals called sophists taught paying customers a variety of subjects and sometimes engaged in what would now be described as research. Aristophanes satirizes sophists'' practices and views as foolish, as seen when the character _____'),
  ('9415e606-52ea-5c74-8d8f-b7badf4677e9', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["iron production may have originated in Anatolia much earlier than the available evidence currently indicates.", "iron production technologies were likely transmitted from Anatolia to Central Africa via an alternate route than the one suggested by some proponents of the single origin hypothesis.", "iron production technologies found in Gbabari likely derived directly from technologies transmitted from Anatolia, but those found in Oboui and Gbatoro did not.", "iron production may have developed independently and relatively simultaneously in Anatolia and parts of Central Africa."]'::jsonb, 14, 'The single origin hypothesis of iron metallurgy posits that the craft originated in Anatolia (West Asia) circa 2200-2000 BCE before diffusing to other parts of the world, including Africa. Some proponents of the hypothesis argue that iron production technologies first arrived in North Africa through Carthage, where the earliest evidence of ironworking dates to approximately 800-600 BCE, before these technologies spread to sub-Saharan Africa over the following centuries. However, excavation of multiple sites on the Adamawa plateau in Central Africa conducted by Etienne Zangato and Augustin Holl uncovered evidence of iron workshops that may have been in operation as late as 900-750 BCE in Gbabari and as early as 2300-1900 BCE in Oboui and Gbatoro. These findings suggest that _____'),
  ('ac1ac9da-8ee1-56b0-9ec2-82f079e5aa7e', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["the effect of supplemental vitamin D on zoo chimpanzees in Spain and other mid-latitude countries can more clearly be observed in summer than in autumn.", "the possibility that zoo chimpanzees in Spain and other mid-latitude countries would benefit from supplemental vitamin D during autumn cannot be excluded.", "vitamin D levels in zoo chimpanzees in Spain and other mid-latitude countries may be higher during autumn than Moittie and colleagues'' data appear to indicate.", "differences in vitamin D supplementation likely explain more of the difference in vitamin D levels across zoo chimpanzees in Spain and other mid-latitude countries than seasonal differences in UVB irradiation do."]'::jsonb, 15, 'Exclusively inhabiting tropical countries such as Ghana, wild chimpanzees lack adaptations to seasonal variations in ultraviolet B (UVB) irradiance from sunlight; since UVB exposure enables vertebrates to synthesize vitamin D, Sophie Moittie and colleagues studied zoo chimpanzees in Spain and other mid-latitude countries to see how vitamin D levels are affected by the seasonal variations in UVB irradiance that occur in those locations. They found that chimpanzees'' vitamin D levels were significantly lower in autumn than in summer and appeared unaffected by oral supplementation of vitamin D administered by zookeepers. Moittie and colleagues point out, however, that supplementation was rare, highly varied, and poorly tracked, and therefore _____'),
  ('d843375e-44f5-5c82-b9b5-121be4dc2ae6', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["the South Side Writers Group provided a valuable forum for Chicago writers to share ideas;", "writers shared ideas at a valuable forum known as the South Side Writers Group in Chicago;", "Chicago was where the South Side Writers Group provided a valuable forum for writers to share ideas;", "Chicago writers in the South Side Writers Group had a valuable forum for sharing ideas;"]'::jsonb, 16, 'Established in 1936 by African American novelist Richard Wright, _____ it would become a vital part of the creative movement known as the Chicago Black Renaissance.'),
  ('6bedc3ff-d722-58e7-a9eb-e4a8697ab206', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["history the", "history, as the", "history, the", "history. The"]'::jsonb, 17, 'The 1948 founding of the American GI Forum and the 1946 Mendez v. Westminster court decision are regarded as important events in US civil rights _____ former establishing a Latino rights advocacy group and the latter legally affirming the rights of Latino students.'),
  ('f2b7bcf1-a9fa-5cb6-bbe5-666a2d7a7f01', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["have been classified", "are classified", "can be classified", "classified"]'::jsonb, 18, 'Trisyllabic words _____ as dactyls in English metrical verse, such as "article" and "sleepier," consist of one stressed syllable followed by two unstressed syllables.'),
  ('f6a259c5-bbf5-5ac9-b518-f9dfb437036f', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["languages; however,", "languages, however,", "languages. However,", "languages, however;"]'::jsonb, 19, 'Scheme is referred to as a compiled programming language because it typically incorporates a compiler —a tool that translates lines of code into executable commands. Compiling isn’t exclusive to certain programming _____ any language can incorporate this tool.'),
  ('2ef4fb3e-f96d-50eb-afef-0f6c0e3ce2ea', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["songs, for instance,", "songs. For instance, while", "songs; for instance,", "songs, for instance, while"]'::jsonb, 20, 'Reena Esmail, an Indian American classical composer, incorporates Indian musical elements into her work. Esmail’s 2013 violin and piano composition Jhula-jhule intertwines the melodies of two Indian folk _____ in TaReKiTa, a 2016 choral composition, Esmail features onomatopoeic notes imitating the sounds of the tabla, a type of Indian drum.'),
  ('589eb81e-8b19-5173-aa80-f8c01ffbd78d', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["remain", "have remained", "remains", "have been remaining"]'::jsonb, 21, 'The Museum of Modern Art’s vast collection of oil paintings, which includes James Ensor’s Tribulations of Saint Anthony and Yasuo Kuniyoshi’s Self-Portrait as a Golf Player, _____ among its most captivating offerings.'),
  ('3639743b-ee6e-546d-9ee3-ff032e2e8894', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Nevertheless,", "Therefore,", "Actually,", "Finally,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'The traditional process of Turkish paper marbling (ebru) generally proceeds like this: First, the artisan fills a shallow tray with a water bath solution. Next, the artisan adds inks or paints to the solution, which can then be manipulated into intricate designs. _____ the artisan slips paper in and out of the liquid, transferring the design onto the paper.'),
  ('e7da9c0f-fdeb-5f59-af9a-f9e0f1da2983', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["To this end,", "In other words,", "In contrast,", "Granted,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'In the early 1900s, sculptor and collector Gertrude Whitney was one of the foremost champions of avant-garde art, helping many US avant-garde artists gain publicity and exposure. _____ Whitney offered to donate more than 500 avant-garde works to be displayed at a leading New York museum, but the museum said no—so she opened her own, the Whitney Museum of American Art.'),
  ('b133734d-a590-5930-8440-b7356eedce09', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Accordingly,", "Moreover,", "To this end,", "That being said,"]'::jsonb, 24, 'The geologic principle of cross-cutting relationships states that an intrusion is younger than the rocks through which it cuts. _____ geophysicists analyzing a given rock formation can ascertain that an igneous intrusion that bisects a layer of 358.9-million-year-old Tournaisian rock but not the 303.7- million-year-old Gzhelian rock above it is younger than the former but older than the latter.'),
  ('57ed4cee-e09e-5b4a-87bd-4a2eec40dcc7', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'The student wants to specify the order in which different government entities were given the authority to regulate airline safety in the US. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["The authority to regulate US airline safety transferred from the US Commerce Department to the CAA in 1938, then from the CAA to the FAA in 1958.", "The CAA had the authority to regulate safety for US airlines from 1938 until 1958, at which point authority was transferred to the US Commerce Department by Elwood R. Quesada.", "The FAA, CAA, and the US Commerce Department all had the authority to regulate US airline safety, but they possessed this authority at different times.", "The FAA has regulated airline safety since it was established by the US Congress in 1958."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

1926: The US Congress gave the US Commerce Department authority to regulate safety standards in the fledgling commercial airline industry.
1938: Congress transferred this authority to a new independent government agency called the Civil Aeronautics Authority (CAA).
1958: Congress transferred authority from the CAA to the newly established Federal Aviation Administration (FAA).
The FAA’s first administrator, Elwood R. Quesada, updated safety standards and technologies for the era of commercial jets.
The FAA remains the regulatory authority for airline safety.'),
  ('3ddd4148-ab00-5f02-a54e-af735ae41d73', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'The student wants to make a generalization about the germination of seeds ingested by bats. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["Seed ingestion by bats can have varying effects on seed germination.", "Over the years, researchers have studied the effect that seed germination has had on ingestion by bats.", "As was found in the 2017 study, ingestion by bats has a negative effect on the germination of plant seeds.", "Bat ingestion has consistently been found to have a positive effect on seed germination."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

R. Oleksy, L. Giuggioli, and T.J. McKetterick published a study in 2017.
In it, the researchers found that ingestion by bats had a positive effect on the germination of Ficus grevei plant seeds.
Jorge E. Lopez and C. Vaughan published a study in 2004.
In it, the researchers found that ingestion by bats had a neutral effect on the germination of Senna fruticosa plant seeds.
J.M. Palmeirim, D.L. Gorchov, and S. Stoleson published a study in 1989.
In it, the researchers found that ingestion by bats had a negative effect on the germination of Piper friedrichsthali plant seeds.'),
  ('8b6eab44-a731-5c83-bee7-5be1d4522101', 'a1dfcd58-4343-5bb0-9901-4ed6346b2f7b', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to explain plosive phonemes?', '["The phoneme /p/, a unit of sound in which air flow is at first fully blocked, has labial articulation.", "The phoneme /p/ in the word \"pan\" is plosive and is produced using both lips.", "The linguistic term of fricative is used for units of sound in which air flow is partially blocked.", "In linguistics, plosive is a term used for units of sound in which air flow is at first fully blocked."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Linguists use phonemes to represent the smallest possible units of sound within a word. In English, the phoneme /p/ has labial articulation, which means it is produced using one or both lips, such as in the word "pan."
The phoneme /z/ has alveolar articulation, which means it is produced by placing the tongue against or near the roof of the mouth, such as in the word "zoo."
/p/ is plosive, a term used for sounds in which air flow is at first fully blocked.
/z/ is fricative, a term used for sounds in which air flow is partially blocked.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('a9ab378d-7928-51c7-a9f8-cc80d84712c7', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence explains that Rosa Guraieb’s compositions "lend themselves to as many different interpretations as there are people to listen to them," so the blank should denote "many" or "countless" meanings; "myriad" precisely conveys that sense of numerous meanings. The parallel structure and the following clause make clear that plurality, not paucity, is intended.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Superficial" means shallow or surface-level; nothing in the passage indicates that the meanings are shallow, only that they are numerous.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Deficient" means lacking; that contradicts the idea that there are many
interpretations.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Untenable" means unable to be defended; that does not match the context about multiplicity of interpretations.
**Faster approach:** Look for context words that indicate quantity (here, "as many different interpretations"), then choose the answer that means "many"—a quick synonym check eliminates the others.'),
  ('565cb9fa-0633-509c-a103-3794f720791d', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** In context, the narrator calls the patch of land “with some exaggeration called a garden,” and then says the flower beds “at first sight confirmed the illusion.” That means the beds made the (false or exaggerated) idea that it was a garden seem true or supported. “Substantiated” matches that sense of providing evidence or support.
**Choice A is incorrect.** “Necessitated” means made necessary; the flower beds did not make the garden necessary, they only made the illusion seem real.
**Choice C is incorrect.** “Designated” means named or labeled; the sentence does not say the beds named the land a garden.
**Choice D is incorrect.** “Promised” implies future expectation; the beds did not guarantee future outcomes, they simply supported the illusion in the present.
**Faster approach:** Replace the word with a synonym in the sentence; if the sentence still makes sense, that synonym is likely correct. Here, substituting “substantiated” preserves the meaning.'),
  ('399f40fc-a6f3-5250-9ca6-7cf4d0cfe40a', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence says the new measurement "differs significantly from the standard model’s prediction," which implies the measurement would call the model’s accuracy into question; to "undermine" a theory means to weaken or cast doubt on it, so this word fits logically and precisely.
**Choice A is incorrect.** To "satisfy" the standard model would mean the measurement agrees with or supports the model, which contradicts the clause that the measurement "differs significantly" from the prediction.
**Choice B is incorrect.** To "simplify" the standard model would mean making it less complex; the sentence describes a conflict between data and prediction, not a reduction in complexity, so this choice is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** To "overtake" suggests surpassing in rank or importance and doesn’t describe the effect of discrepant empirical data on a theoretical model; it is not a precise fit for the intended meaning.
**Faster approach:** Note the negative implication of "differs significantly from the prediction" and choose the option that conveys weakening or discrediting (a negative effect) rather than confirming or changing complexity—that yields "undermine."'),
  ('9a40065e-7a3a-5ef2-bb4e-31c18cafd2e0', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage says critics “maintain a rigid distinction between the actor and the character,” so they take care never to treat the two as the same. To “conflate” means to combine or mistake two things for one, which is exactly what the author says they avoid.
**Choice B is incorrect.** To “epitomize” means to be a perfect example of something; that does not express the idea of mistakenly merging two identities and therefore doesn’t fit the context.
**Choice C is incorrect.** To “lambaste” means to criticize harshly; the sentence concerns preserving a distinction, not criticizing, so this choice is irrelevant.
**Choice D is incorrect.** To “overshadow” means to outshine or eclipse; while not desirable, it does not convey the specific error of treating actor and character as the same, so it is less precise than “conflate.”
**Faster approach:** Spot the cue word “distinction” and choose the option that means “treat as the same” (conflate) — elimination of unrelated verbs is usually quickest.'),
  ('3c0e0442-7d9b-5c7d-8161-414b338d4fce', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence argues that the public’s understanding of history is essential to a functioning electorate, so historical matters should not be confined to academics; describing history as "critical" fits that idea because it conveys necessity and public importance.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Accessible" means easy to understand; saying history is "too accessible to leave to historians" would imply the opposite of the passage’s point and makes no logical sense here.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Complex" is true in some contexts, but the passage emphasizes the public importance of history rather than its difficulty; "complex" does not explain why historians shouldn’t have sole control.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Respectable" refers to being socially esteemed; that does not connect to the argument about public comprehension and civic function, so it fails to complete the thought.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each option into the sentence and ask whether it explains why history shouldn’t be left only to historians; the choice that expresses public importance or necessity ("critical") will be correct.'),
  ('51b8bd97-1c24-5516-ae55-7f537298b77c', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage begins with specific Muscogee words (hatki and hathaki), then defines the general linguistic phenomenon (reduplication) that those words exemplify, and concludes by noting that this phenomenon occurs frequently in Muscogee. This sequence—specific example, general definition, frequency statement—matches choice D.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not identify the most frequent Muscogee words, discuss translation difficulty, or provide translations into other languages; it focuses on a morphological process instead.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does explain reduplication but does not discuss any scholarly controversy or argue that Muscogee analysis would resolve such a debate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not compare Muscogee to other languages, pose a question about those relationships, or answer such a question; it treats an internal feature of Muscogee vocabulary.
**Faster approach:** Look for the passage order—example(s) then definition then general comment—and match that sequence to the choice that mirrors it; this quick structural mapping works for every passage-structure question.'),
  ('0d78f499-d099-5259-9b9d-72fdd817acfe', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The underlined sentence describes earlier research that investigated one way convergent evolution can arise, and the next sentences contrast that work with other researchers'' approaches and then describe a later study motivated by the incomplete picture. Thus the underlined sentence functions as an example of how some scientists had studied the phenomenon before the later study was conducted.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although the sentence names specific researchers, it does not promise that those researchers will be discussed in greater detail later; it simply summarizes their prior work.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The sentence does not claim that a later study was influenced by the named researchers; the passage later refers generally to a motivation for a separate study, not direct influence.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While the sentence includes a brief definition of convergent evolution, its primary function is to report prior research, not to clarify an ambiguity in other studies.
**Faster approach:** For questions about a sentence’s function, check verb tense and connector words (e.g., "have explored," "however," "this motivated") to see whether the sentence is presenting
background/example, contrast, or consequence.'),
  ('f7f57e94-a2f5-585a-af06-f46ade0e0dd7', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage states that regional endotherms have a relatively high proportion (greater than 30%) of compact myocardial tissue and that Dolton et al. found basking sharks have 47% compact tissue; the sentence concludes that this finding "undermin[es] that classification," indicating that the percentage by itself is sufficient to call the prior ectotherm categorization into question.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage says the 47% finding undermines the prior classification, so it treats the percentage as sufficient to challenge that categorization, not insufficient.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage gives no direct comparison between the basking shark’s percentage and the salmon shark’s percentage, so we cannot conclude it is higher than the salmon shark’s.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Similarly, the passage does not state the Pacific bluefin tuna’s compact myocardial percentage, so it does not support a claim that the basking shark’s percentage is higher than that of the tuna.
**Faster approach:** Identify the general rule (regional endotherms >30% compact tissue) and compare the given value (47%); if it exceeds the threshold, select the answer that says this evidence overturns or undermines the prior classification.'),
  ('5ad37b62-4c3b-544f-8e33-f75874e4b8f1', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage summarizes Pallikkathayil’s proposal as a way to ground opposition to lying: because false beliefs constrain a person’s agency, we have a duty to respect others'' agential interests that generally requires promoting true beliefs, with exceptions when telling the truth would cause a greater constraint or an otherwise impermissible end. This captures the passage’s main idea—that Pallikkathayil offers an agency-based justification for truthfulness, permitting exceptions.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage explicitly allows exceptions when preventing a greater constraint on agency, so it does not claim an inviolable duty to speak truthfully.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not claim that grounds for opposing lying are unclear; instead it presents Pallikkathayil’s account as a specific ground.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage does not discuss people lacking a principled way to identify permissible circumstances; it asks what grounds opposition to lying and presents Pallikkathayil’s answer, rather than framing the issue as a general lack of such principles.
**Faster approach:** Identify the author’s main claim about the philosopher named (Pallikkathayil) and choose the option that restates that claim (agency-based duty with exceptions); eliminate choices that add claims not in the passage (e.g., ''inviolable duty'' or a different problem setup).'),
  ('dc1fe7b4-9a39-5804-8577-3c7e2617e9da', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The first team found that the location of greatest pressure difference shifts closer to the snout for narrower heads and farther back for wider heads. The second team hypothesizes that sensors at those locations (where information is greatest) are more sensitive. Choice A reports that the longnose gar has a much narrower head and that its most sensitive sensors are closer to the snout than those of the wider-headed devil catfish, which directly links predicted information-rich locations (based on head shape) with greater sensitivity.
**Choice B is incorrect.** If the most sensitive sensors for both species are very close to their snouts, that does not match the prediction that wider heads should have their information-rich sensors farther back, so it does not support the hypothesis that sensitivity corresponds to information-rich locations.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This reports that only the narrow-headed species shows higher sensitivity at the snout; the wide-headed species shows no sensor variation. That fails to show the predicted alignment between information-rich locations and sensitivity for both species and thus does not directly support the general hypothesis.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This contradicts the first team’s finding: it says the wider-headed devil catfish has its most sensitive sensors closer to the snout than the narrower longnose gar, which would undermine rather than support the hypothesis.
**Faster approach:** Match the predicted information-rich location (based on head width/length) to reported sensor sensitivity—if the locations coincide, the choice supports the hypothesis; if they don’t or are the same for both species, it does not.'),
  ('ab0f8920-e034-5004-9ffc-e9c7fe215be7', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The table shows geothermal at 2,131 MW in both 2019 and 2020 and wind at 154.3 MW in both 2019 and 2020, so neither geothermal nor wind changed between those two years. Those specific numbers from the table directly support the assertion that "both geothermal and wind neither increased nor decreased from 2019 to 2020."
**Choice B is incorrect.** While wind and solar never exceed renewable hydropower in 2017–2020 (hydropower values are 5,703–6,141 MW, far above wind and solar), the choice asserts both wind and solar "never surpassed that of renewable hydropower" — this is true but is a less precise fit because the passage focuses on the 2019–2020 comparison just described; moreover, B is a broader statement not tied to the immediate trend described and thus is not the most effective completion compared with the exact 2019–2020 equality shown in the table.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although wind was much lower in 2017 (1.5 MW) than in 2018–2020 (143.5 and 154.3 MW), this choice restates a single-year increase rather than the specific 2019–2020 lack of change referenced in the passage; it does not complete the sentence as precisely as A.
**Choice D is incorrect.** While renewable hydropower is indeed much higher than solar in every year (hydropower 5,703–6,141 MW versus solar 65.5–185.3 MW), like B and C this statement is a broader comparative claim and does not directly address the 2019–2020 stability the passage highlights; A is the direct, data-supported completion.
**Faster approach:** Scan the specific years named in the sentence (here, 2019 and 2020) and check the table entries for those years only; if the numbers are identical for a technology, you can immediately select the choice asserting no change. This method reliably answers evidence questions that compare specific years.'),
  ('ce42085c-d751-5b71-b6f3-5a82aba2547c', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The passage reports that spacing products far apart generally increases perceived value but that a store context associated with inexpensive products may moderate that effect. Choice C states that the spacing effect (far apart judged more valuable than close together) is strong at Tiffany & Co. but much weaker at Forever 21, which directly indicates that the inexpensive-store context reduces (moderates) the spacing effect.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It reports the opposite ordering (far apart judged less valuable than close together) and thus conflicts with the basic effect described in the passage.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It describes a crossover interaction where spacing reverses the relative value judgments between stores, which is more complex than the moderation the passage suggests and is not the simplest support for reduced effect in an inexpensive context.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It says far-apart items are judged similarly across stores while close-together items differ, which does not indicate that the spacing effect itself is weaker at the inexpensive retailer.
**Faster approach:** Look for an answer that shows the spacing effect is present but smaller at the inexpensive store (i.e., a bigger spacing difference at the upscale store than at the inexpensive one).'),
  ('dc41bd0c-4625-588f-9e27-80fee202df9f', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The quotation shows a sophist’s study method as absurd — suspending himself in a basket and claiming that doing so lets him ‘‘rightly discover things celestial’’ — which directly supports the claim that Aristophanes satirizes sophists as foolish. The vivid, ridiculous image in the line provides clear evidence of ridicule.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The line is vague and could be read as confidential or instructional rather than plainly mocking sophistic practices; it does not depict foolishness.
**Choice B is incorrect.** That polite request to ‘‘learn’’ from someone doesn’t portray sophists as foolish; it’s neutral and fails to illustrate satire.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although it suggests opportunism, the line is more about motivation than showing the sophists’ practices as absurd or ridiculous; it’s less directly satirical than D.
**Faster approach:** For evidence questions about satire or mockery, pick the quotation that depicts a concrete, ridiculous action or image (a caricature) rather than a neutral or merely critical statement.'),
  ('9415e606-52ea-5c74-8d8f-b7badf4677e9', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage gives dates for Anatolia (circa 2200–2000 BCE) and for Central African sites (as early as 2300–1900 BCE), which overlap and in some ranges precede the Anatolian dates. That temporal overlap supports the conclusion that iron production could have developed independently and at about the same time in both regions rather than stemming from a single Anatolian origin.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The evidence in Central Africa does not specifically imply that Anatolia’s iron production began earlier than currently dated; it instead shows that comparable ironworking existed in Central Africa, which undermines the need to push Anatolian dates earlier.
**Choice B is incorrect.** While the passage challenges the Carthage-transmission route for North Africa, it does not provide evidence identifying an alternate transmission path from Anatolia to Central Africa; the earlier Central African dates better support independent development than a different diffusion route.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage gives no basis for selectively attributing Gbabari’s later ironworking to Anatolian transmission while denying such a connection for the much earlier Oboui and Gbatoro sites; that assertion is unsupported by the provided dates.
**Faster approach:** Compare the chronological ranges given for each region; if dates overlap or Central Africa is as early or earlier, favor answers proposing independent or simultaneous development rather than a single-source diffusion.'),
  ('ac1ac9da-8ee1-56b0-9ec2-82f079e5aa7e', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The authors note that supplementation was rare, highly varied, and poorly tracked, which creates uncertainty about any effects of supplementation; therefore they cannot rule out the possibility that chimpanzees might benefit from supplemental vitamin D in autumn. This completion directly follows from the admission of inadequate data.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Nothing in the passage suggests that supplementation’s effects would be easier to observe in summer than in autumn; the passage emphasizes lack of reliable supplementation data, not season-specific observability.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage reports lower autumn levels, and poor tracking of supplementation does not imply the measured autumn values are systematically higher than reported.
**Choice D is incorrect.** If supplementation was poorly tracked and varied, one cannot conclude it explains more of the differences than seasonal UVB; that claim overstates what the data support.
**Faster approach:** Note language of limitation (e.g., "rare," "poorly tracked") — such qualifiers typically lead to a conclusion that a possibility cannot be ruled out; choose the answer expressing that uncertainty.'),
  ('d843375e-44f5-5c82-b9b5-121be4dc2ae6', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The introductory participial phrase "Established in 1936 by African American novelist Richard Wright," must modify the noun phrase that immediately follows; choice A begins with that noun phrase, "the South Side Writers Group," so the modifier attaches properly. The sentence then continues with a second independent clause after the semicolon: the group provided a valuable forum, and it would become a vital part of the Chicago Black Renaissance.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The phrase would make "writers" the subject modified by "Established in 1936...," which illogically implies the writers (not the group) were established in 1936.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Beginning with "Chicago" would misattach the modifier so that the city is presented as having been established in 1936, which is incorrect.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Putting "Chicago writers" immediately after the modifier similarly makes the writers, not the writers'' group, the thing established in 1936, producing a dangling/misplaced modifier.
**Faster approach:** Use modifier placement: the phrase must immediately precede the noun it describes—eliminate any choice that puts a person or place (writers, Chicago) right after the introductory participle.'),
  ('6bedc3ff-d722-58e7-a9eb-e4a8697ab206', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence requires a punctuation mark that links the main clause to a nonrestrictive participial phrase that explains how those events were important: ", the former establishing... and the latter..." correctly sets off that explanatory phrase. The comma introduces the descriptive phrase without creating a fragment.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It lacks necessary punctuation; "history the former..." creates a run-on and is ungrammatical.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "history, as the former..." makes "as" a needless subordinating word and produces an awkward, ungrammatical construction following "as."
**Choice D is incorrect.** A period before "The former..." produces a sentence fragment because "The former establishing..." is not an independent clause.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the final clause is an explanatory participial phrase; if so, use a comma plus the phrase (", the ...") rather than a bare noun or a period that would create a fragment.'),
  ('f2b7bcf1-a9fa-5cb6-bbe5-666a2d7a7f01', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The past-participial modifier ''classified'' creates a reduced relative clause modifying ''Trisyllabic words,'' so the sentence reads smoothly as ''Trisyllabic words classified as dactyls ... consist of...'' That yields a single main verb (''consist'') and correctly links the descriptive phrase to the noun.
**Choice A is incorrect.** ''have been classified'' is a full verb phrase; inserted here it would create two finite verbs (''have been classified'' and ''consist'') without proper coordination, making the sentence ungrammatical and altering aspect.
**Choice B is incorrect.** ''are classified'' likewise produces a separate finite clause and would require punctuation or a conjunction to join it to ''consist.''
**Choice C is incorrect.** ''can be classified'' adds an unwarranted modal of possibility and again creates a second independent verb phrase.
**Faster approach:** If the sentence already contains a main verb after the blank, the blank usually should be a modifier (a past participle) rather than another finite verb; choose the participial form to attach the description to the noun.'),
  ('f6a259c5-bbf5-5ac9-b518-f9dfb437036f', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence structure calls for a continuation of the thought after "languages". Using "however;" appropriately connects the two independent clauses, indicating a contrast while maintaining grammatical correctness. The semicolon suggests a pause before introducing an alternative idea related to the first part of the sentence.
Now, let’s analyze the other options:
**Choice A:** "languages; however," is incorrect because the semicolon before "however" is unnecessary and breaks the sentence flow. A comma should be used instead to keep it fluid.
**Choice B:** "languages, however," incorrectly uses a comma instead of a semicolon. Although grammatically acceptable, it lacks the appropriate pause needed between two independent clauses. **Choice C:** "languages. However," uses a period to separate the clauses, which results in a fragment following "However". It disrupts the continuity of the thought presented in the sentence.'),
  ('2ef4fb3e-f96d-50eb-afef-0f6c0e3ce2ea', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence needs punctuation that allows the clause beginning with "while" to properly modify the second example (TaReKiTa) without creating a sentence fragment. "songs, for instance, while" supplies a parenthetical "for instance" set off by commas and directly connects the continuing clause "while Esmail features..." to the main clause, yielding a single grammatically complete sentence: "Reena Esmail ... intertwines the melodies of two Indian folk songs, for instance, while in TaReKiTa, Esmail features..." This preserves the intended contrast/continuation and keeps "while Esmail features..." attached to the main sentence rather than forming an independent fragment.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "songs, for instance," ends the phrase correctly but leaves the following "while Esmail features..." as a sentence fragment because there is no main verb to complete a new sentence; the comma alone does not link the "while" clause properly.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "songs. For instance, while" creates a new sentence that begins with "For instance, while..." This leaves the clause incomplete: "while Esmail features..." becomes a dependent clause attached to no independent clause, producing a fragment.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "songs; for instance," uses a semicolon to join independent clauses, but what follows ("while Esmail features...") is a dependent clause introduced by "while" and cannot correctly follow a semicolon; this also yields a fragment or a mismatched construction.
**Faster approach:** When a sentence continues with "while" or another subordinating conjunction, make sure that punctuation attaches that subordinate clause to an independent clause—look for commas setting off parenthetical phrases ("for instance,") and avoid ending the sentence before the subordinating clause, which creates a fragment.'),
  ('589eb81e-8b19-5173-aa80-f8c01ffbd78d', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The subject of the sentence is the singular noun phrase "The Museum of Modern Art’s vast collection of oil paintings," so the verb must be singular. "Remains" is the singular present-tense verb that agrees with that subject and fits the nonrestrictive clause "which includes..."
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Remain" is a plural verb form and does not agree with the singular subject "collection."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Have remained" is plural and also changes the tense/aspect in a way that conflicts with the simple present sense of the sentence.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Have been remaining" is ungrammatical here: it is plural and an awkward progressive perfect that is not appropriate.
**Faster approach:** Identify the main subject (singular "collection") and choose the singular present verb, so pick "remains."'),
  ('3639743b-ee6e-546d-9ee3-ff032e2e8894', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage lists steps in order: filling a tray, adding and manipulating paints, and then transferring the design. "Finally," signals the last step in a sequence, so it fits logically and maintains the chronological flow.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Nevertheless," indicates contrast or an unexpected result, but the sentence continues the procedural sequence rather than contradicting it.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Therefore," indicates a cause-and-effect conclusion; the sentence describes the next step in the process, not a result drawn from the previous actions.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Actually," expresses correction or surprise and does not signal the final step of a series, so it disrupts the clear procedural sequence.
**Faster approach:** For sequence questions, look for chronological cues (First, Next) and choose a transition that continues the order (e.g., Finally) rather than one indicating contrast or cause.'),
  ('e7da9c0f-fdeb-5f59-af9a-f9e0f1da2983', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence before the blank describes Whitney’s efforts to promote avant-garde artists; "To this end," signals that the following action was taken for that purpose. Reading the full sentence as "To this end, Whitney offered to donate more than 500 avant-garde works..." makes clear that the donation was intended to further her goal of helping avant-garde artists, which fits the logical sequence leading to her opening a museum when refused.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "In other words" would restate or clarify the previous idea, but the sentence that follows reports a specific action (an offer to donate) rather than a paraphrase, so this transition is inappropriate.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "In contrast" indicates opposition between clauses; here the donation offer continues and supports the previous statement rather than contrasting with it.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Granted," introduces a concession or qualification, which does not match the causal/purpose relationship between promoting artists and offering the donation.
**Faster approach:** For purpose/result relationships, pick transitions like "To this end," or "Therefore"; for restatement, choose "In other words;" for opposition, choose "In contrast." Identifying the intended relationship between sentences quickly narrows choices.'),
  ('b133734d-a590-5930-8440-b7356eedce09', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence before the blank states a general principle about relative ages of intrusions and surrounding rocks; the next clause applies that principle to geophysicists'' conclusions, so a consequence marker is required. "Accordingly," signals that what follows is a direct result of the stated principle, making it the logical transition.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Moreover," adds additional information rather than indicating a conclusion drawn from the preceding statement, so it does not properly signal the result-oriented relationship here.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "To this end," means "for this purpose" and implies intent or goal-directed action; the sentence describes an inference, not a purpose, so this choice is inappropriate.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "That being said," introduces a contrast or exception to the previous statement, but the next clause is not contrasting the principle—it’s applying it—so this transition is wrong.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the second clause presents a consequence (use
"Accordingly/Therefore"), an addition ("Moreover"), a purpose ("To this end"), or a contrast ("That being said"); pick the word matching the relationship.'),
  ('57ed4cee-e09e-5b4a-87bd-4a2eec40dcc7', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The notes give specific dates showing the sequence of authority: in 1926 the US Commerce Department was given authority, in 1938 Congress transferred that authority to the Civil Aeronautics Authority (CAA), and in 1958 Congress transferred authority from the CAA to the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA). Choice A accurately summarizes that chronological transfer from the Commerce Department to the CAA in 1938 and then from the CAA to the FAA in 1958.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It reverses the transfer in 1958 and incorrectly claims authority was transferred back to the US Commerce Department by Elwood R. Quesada, which contradicts the notes.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although generally true that each entity had authority at different times, it fails to specify the order—so it does not accomplish the student’s goal of specifying the sequence.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It states that the FAA has regulated safety since its 1958 establishment but omits the prior transfers from the Commerce Department to the CAA and so does not specify the full order.
**Faster approach:** Create a brief timeline from the dated notes (1926 → 1938 → 1958) and choose the answer that lists entities in that chronological order.'),
  ('3ddd4148-ab00-5f02-a54e-af735ae41d73', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The notes report three studies with different outcomes: one found a positive effect (Ficus greveii), one found a neutral effect (Senna fruticosa), and one found a negative effect (Piper friedrichsthal). Summarizing these results, the most accurate generalization is that seed ingestion by bats can have varying effects on germination.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It reverses the causal relationship described in the notes: the studies examined the effect of bat ingestion on seed germination, not the effect of seed germination on bat ingestion.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The 2017 study reported a positive effect, not a negative one; this choice misstates that study’s result.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The studies do not show consistent positive effects; the notes include neutral and negative findings as well, so "consistently positive" is unsupported.
**Faster approach:** Scan the brief summaries for the outcome words (positive, neutral, negative); if you see more than one type, choose the option that indicates variation or mixed results.'),
  ('8b6eab44-a731-5c83-bee7-5be1d4522101', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** It restates the definition given in the notes: plosive is a term for sounds in which airflow is at first fully blocked. This choice directly explains what plosive phonemes are without adding extra or potentially misleading detail.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Although it uses information about /p/ and plosives, it presents /p/ itself as the unit defined rather than stating the general definition of plosive phonemes; it is less clear and more specific than the general explanation requested.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It ties the plosive characteristic to the specific example /p/ and asserts production with "both lips," which is narrower than the general explanation of what plosive phonemes are.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It defines fricative rather than explaining what plosive phonemes are, so it does not answer the question.
**Faster approach:** For definition questions, pick the choice that states the general definition given in the passage rather than one that focuses on a single example.')
